<section>
	<div class="container py-5">
        <div class="row justify-content-center">
            <div class="col-12 text-center">
                <p class="fw-semibold mb-5">Organisations often experience transformation problems as technology problems.</p>
                <p class="mb-5 fw-bold">A platform isn't delivering, adoption is slow, decisions take too long, teams disagree about priorities, a programme loses momentum.</p>
                <p class="mb-5 fw-semibold">But the visible problem isn't always the real problem.</p>
                <p class="fw-bold mb-0">I use four lenses to understand what's actually getting in the way: Decisions, Leadership, Technology and Change.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="row py-5 border-top">
            <div class="col-md-3">
                <h3 class="fw-semibold">
                    <span class="material-symbols-rounded me-2">psychology_alt</span>
                    Decisions
                </h3>
                <p class="fs-5">How are decisions actually made?</p>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-9">
                <p>Who owns them? How quickly are they made? Which decisions keep getting deferred? What happens when information is incomplete?</p>

                <p>Transformation requires organisations to make thousands of decisions. When ownership is unclear, information is fragmented or people are afraid to make trade-offs, technology rarely solves the problem.</p>

                <p>The question:</p>
                <p>What is preventing the organisation from making the decisions it needs to make?</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="row py-5 border-top">
            <div class="col-md-3">
                <h3 class="fw-semibold">
                    <span class="material-symbols-rounded me-2">explore</span>
                    Leadership
                </h3>
                <p class="fs-5">What conditions are leaders creating?</p>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-9">
                <p>Is there trust? Are people empowered? Is disagreement safe? Are priorities clear? Are leaders enabling decisions or adding another layer of control?</p>

                <p>Leadership shapes the environment in which transformation happens. The behaviours an organisation rewards can matter as much as its formal structures and processes.</p>

                <p>The question:</p>
                <p>Are leadership behaviours enabling change or constraining it?</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="row py-5 border-top">
            <div class="col-md-3">
                <h3 class="fw-semibold">
                    <span class="material-symbols-rounded me-2">memory</span>
                    Technology
                </h3>
                <p class="fs-5">What is the technology revealing?</p>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-9">
                <p>Where is it exposing poor processes, unclear ownership or fragmented information? Is the technology genuinely the constraint?</p>

                <p>New technology often makes existing organisational weaknesses more visible. Automating a poor process doesn't necessarily improve it. Connecting fragmented teams doesn't automatically create collaboration.</p>

                <p>The question:</p>
                <p>Is technology the problem, or is it exposing something deeper?</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="row py-5 border-top">
            <div class="col-md-3">
                <h3 class="fw-semibold">
                    <span class="material-symbols-rounded me-2">sync_alt</span>
                    Change
                </h3>
                <p class="fs-5">Does the change make sense to the people experiencing it?</p>
            </div>

            <div class="col-md-9">
                <p>What additional complexity is being created during the transition? Do people understand why the change matters? Are they being given permission and time to learn?</p>

                <p>People don't simply need to be told about change. They need to make sense of what it means for their work.</p>

                <p>The question:</p>
                <p>What would need to be true for this change to make sense to the people affected by it?</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>